---
import DocLayout from "../layouts/DocLayout.astro";
import CodeBlock from "../components/CodeBlock.astro";
import Callout from "../components/Callout.astro";
---

<DocLayout title="Customization" description="Restyle, rename, and add actions without forking.">
  <h1>Customization</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Four levels, from cheapest to most involved. None of them require modifying the library.
  </p>

  <h2>1 · CSS variables</h2>

  <p>The right answer for almost everything visual. <a href="/themes">Full token list</a>.</p>

  <CodeBlock
    lang="css"
    code={`:root {
  --tat-color-primary: #059669;
  --tat-tooltip-width: 360px;
  --tat-radius-lg: 4px;
  --tat-shadow-lg: 0 20px 50px -16px rgb(0 0 0 / 0.28);
}`}
  />

  <h2>2 · Extra class names</h2>

  <CodeBlock
    lang="js"
    code={`createTour({
  tourId: "x",
  classes: {
    root: "acme-tour",
    overlay: "acme-tour__scrim",
    spotlight: "acme-tour__spotlight",
    tooltip: "acme-tour__panel",
    modal: "acme-tour__dialog",
    btn: "acme-btn",
    btnPrimary: "acme-btn--primary",
  },
  steps: [/* … */],
});`}
  />

  <p>
    Your classes are merged with the library's, never replacing them, so nothing can break by
    adding one.
  </p>

  <h2>3 · Targeted CSS</h2>

  <p>
    Once you have your own class on a component, ordinary specificity does the rest:
  </p>

  <CodeBlock
    lang="css"
    code={`.acme-tour__panel {
  border-radius: 2px;
  font-family: "Inter", sans-serif;
}

.acme-tour__panel .tat-btn--primary {
  background: #059669;
  border-color: #059669;
}`}
  />

  <h2>4 · Replacing a component's content</h2>

  <p>
    For structural changes, stop fighting CSS. Custom actions, function-valued
    <code>title</code>/<code>content</code>, and per-step options cover most needs.
  </p>

  <CodeBlock
    lang="js"
    code={`{
  tourId: "home.cta",

  // Computed at render time from the step context.
  title: (ctx) => \`Step \${ctx.position + 1}\`,
  content: (ctx) => \`\${ctx.total} steps in this tour.\`,

  actions: [
    { label: "Open settings", onClick: () => router.push("/settings") },
    { label: "Book a call", primary: true, onClick: () => window.open(calLink) },
  ],
}`}
  />

  <h2>Labelling</h2>

  <CodeBlock
    lang="js"
    code={`createTour({
  tourId: "x",
  labels: {
    previous: "Zurück",
    next: "Weiter",
    done: "Fertig",
    skip: "Überspringen",
    close: "Schließen",
    stepLabel: "Schritt",
    progress: "Schritt {current} von {total}",
  },
  steps: [/* … */],
});`}
  />

  <p>
    Every visible string is configurable, and every control's accessible name comes from the same
    labels — so localising once covers both the screen and the screen reader.
  </p>

  <h2>Disabling pieces</h2>

  <CodeBlock
    lang="js"
    code={`createTour({
  tourId: "x",
  spotlight: false,          // no dimming, no cut-out
  showProgress: false,       // no progress bar
  showCloseButton: false,    // no close control
  showStepCounter: true,     // counter instead of the bar
  closeOnEscape: false,      // Escape does nothing
  closeOnOverlayClick: true, // backdrop click ends the tour
  keyboardNavigation: false, // keyboard is inert
  animation: "none",         // no transitions at all
  steps: [/* … */],
});`}
  />

  <h2>Custom actions, deeply</h2>

  <p>
    An action's <code>onClick</code> receives the event and may return a value. It runs before the
    tour advances, so returning normally lets the default behaviour continue.
  </p>

  <CodeBlock
    lang="js"
    code={`{
  tourId: "home.cta",
  title: "Create a project",
  actions: [
    {
      label: "Create one now",
      primary: true,
      onClick: (event) => {
        event.preventDefault();
        openCreateModal();
        // The tour keeps running; close it yourself if that is what you want.
      },
    },
  ],
}`}
  />

  <Callout title="No render hooks">
    <p>
      There is deliberately no slot or render hook in this version. Custom actions plus per-step
      content cover the real cases, and a hook system would put a second, undocumented extension
      surface into a library whose main promise is that the documented API is the whole API.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2>What is not supported</h2>

  <ul>
    <li>Swapping the visual language entirely — that is a fork, and the honest answer.</li>
    <li>Injecting markup from configuration — use <code>unsafeHTML</code> deliberately, or not at all.</li>
    <li>Overriding individual z-index layers — move the whole stack with <code>--tat-z</code>.</li>
  </ul>
</DocLayout>